Include <sys/ucontext.h> only where the system provides it.

The include sits in the header's catch-all arm, on the assumption that every
Unix that is not OpenBSD or QNX carries the header.  This one does not: its
kernel hands signal handlers no context argument at all (the third argument
of an SA_SIGINFO handler is NULL), so the C library would have nothing
truthful to put in such a header, and inventing types that describe no real
kernel object would be worse than the missing file.

Everything that DEREFERENCES a context in this header lives behind
HAVE(MACHINE_CONTEXT), which is off for this platform: the build falls
through to the portable PlatformRegisters -- a bare stack pointer -- and the
thread suspension machinery in ThreadingPOSIX.cpp uses its approximate-stack-
pointer path, which is exactly the arrangement the other no-machine-context
ports run.  __has_include keeps the arm accurate anywhere rather than naming
this system.

Upstream: not reported yet (portability fix; candidate for upstreaming).

--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/PlatformRegisters.h
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/PlatformRegisters.h
@@ -39,8 +39,10 @@ typedef ucontext_t mcontext_t;
 #elif OS(QNX)
 #include <ucontext.h>
-#else
+#elif __has_include(<sys/ucontext.h>)
 #include <sys/ucontext.h>
+#else
+/* No context reaches a signal handler here; the portable arm below applies. */
 #endif

 namespace WTF {
